--TEST--
`secret` is refused at both of the graph copy's carriers and by one rule: `spawn script`'s `args:` and `Core\Serialize::encode` report the same code for the same value, whatever route it arrived by, while a plain or `tainted` value crosses — `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`, `rule:classes/graph-copy`, `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`
--FILE child.nvs--
<?nvs
return 1;
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:classes/graph-copy` defines one graph copy with two carriers — arena-to-arena at
// the isolate boundary, and to bytes and back — and `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse` refuses a
// `secret` value "at the one recursive graph-copy operation `rule:classes/two-copy-depths` already
// defines, for both its callers alike, rather than drawing a new distinction
// between crossing to a live isolate and externalizing to bytes."
//
// The neighbouring case pins the byte carrier's own routes. This one is the
// agreement (`docs/agent/conventions.md`): the same value handed to the two
// carriers must be refused with the same code, so a carrier that grew its own
// reading of the qualifier fails here while still looking right alone.
class Vault {
    public static function token(): secret string {
        return "sk-live";
    }
}

string $plain = "plain";
tainted string $t = "t";
secret string $s = "s";
secret bytes $sb = "s" as bytes;
secret tainted string $st = "s";

// The accepted side first, so this is a bound with both sides named. `tainted`
// is not on § 4's list at this sink: taint says who *wrote* the value, and the
// child is no less entitled to it than the parent is.
spawn script "child.nvs" with(args: $plain);
spawn script "child.nvs" with(args: $t);

// Carrier one, directly.
spawn script "child.nvs" with(args: $s);

// Composed rather than declared: the qualifier poisons through concatenation
// on its own axis, so what the site reads is the argument's inferred type.
spawn script "child.nvs" with(args: "bearer " . $s);

// The other base, and the two bits together — `secret` is one axis and
// `tainted` never launders it.
spawn script "child.nvs" with(args: $sb);
spawn script "child.nvs" with(args: $st);

// Declared one frame away, which is where a rule that only looked at variables
// would miss it.
spawn script "child.nvs" with(args: Vault::token());

// Carrier two, with the same value. Same code, and a message that differs only
// in the clause naming where the copy went.
bytes $out = Core\Serialize::encode($s);
echo Core\Bytes::length($out);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `spawn script`'s `args:` copies it into a child whose arena this request cannot reach into, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
